      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/observations/step-q159266-fd4eb28f65983a27e8a5.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/observations/step-q159266-fd4eb28f65983a27e8a5.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/tasks/14134473/topics/1930/lesson",
      "captured_at": 1791803845.7068398,
      "math_academy_id": "q-159266",
      "topic_id": "1930",
      "sequence_position": null,
      "source_step": {
        "dom_id": "step-q159266",
        "placement_id": null,
        "content_id": null,
        "type": null
      },
      "source_example_id": "e-10924",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers",
      "kp_id": "e-10924"
    },
    "q-159276": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159276",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/7cb1268b1007611ba22364c23439e329f33d806a585bffb497b40a0c1e5f316e.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-2,1)\\cup (1,3)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/bb77fe9590ad2dfd4ff0259da48765eb3539e58590f585f4530d121aea1553ee.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $B$ does not belong to $M$ and $B$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $B.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/1dc73455fa8f3a82dded795ccb1e375fe32765f2279a654f1694e8b4cb5aed7f.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/4f9ed1e3fa6b3232cb8f37d756e11034563722080e769b351d0e4ebeb0c4ecc3.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
