    "q-158562": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158562",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<1-2x\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=1-2x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(-1,3)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(1,-1)$ is an interior point of $A$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=1-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/7ce1ca6af3caf2b454d7fed484f98d11478839bcf107c0f19562688c7cdaca6a.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-1,3)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/aad8bc7bfd64af0dd1e08618626ae3237bb4aba42be1f91fe7ff9328ad6ecbe7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ satisfy $y=1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
