    "q-105334": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105334",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n\nII. The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ is a boundary point of $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(2,2)$ is an interior point of $A$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/a1371f9abdeae5083171f5ddfa5d3e237b7e223ba2ba5cf8a822322c34a4676c.png)\n\n \n\nLet's add the points $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(2,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/021ebd260ca399ee47bf8268d02a1a05019d63eef8ab2f5facc56f8576195a80.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ do not satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
